Researching activity/audit data model design for Humility platform...

[1/5] Problem framing — actions vs state changes
  Analyzed semantic split between attempted operations (actions) and
  accepted business facts (state changes). Concluded the split avoids
  co-mingling failed writes with accepted writes in audit streams.

[2/5] Entity-row versioning vs activity-derived versioning
  Compared current ChapterRead/ChapterUpdate row-level version pattern
  against activity-derived ETag model. Activity-derived versioning
  decouples concurrency, history, and business state from entity rows.
  Cost: higher write-path complexity, mandatory projections.

[3/5] Activity-table schema options
  Evaluated three schema shapes:
  - Option A: single append-only table (simple, but conflates actions and state changes)
  - Option B: split activity_actions + activity_state_changes (clean separation)
  - Option C: Option B + projection/head helpers (practical end state)
  Recommended Option C as the operational target with Option B as the conceptual model.

[4/5] Optimistic locking via ETag / head hash
  Evaluated sequence-only vs state-hash validators. Recommended per-aggregate
  head hash carried by accepted state-change stream, exposed as opaque ETag.
  API contract: If-Match for mutations, 412 Precondition Failed for stale writes.

[5/5] Migration impact on chapters API
  Documented contract break: version leaves ChapterRead/ChapterUpdate,
  created_at becomes history metadata, stale updates move from 409 to 412,
  repository pattern changes from replace(..., version+1) to append-action +
  append-state-change + advance-head + refresh-projection.

